 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Relates to the creation of micro-business worker cooperatives upon transfer of ownership.",
 "title": "Relates to the creation of micro-business worker cooperatives upon transfer of ownership",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A2804",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A2804-2017",
     "printNo": "A2804",
     "session": 2017,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 274\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 2804-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the New York state urban development corporation\n      act and the economic development law, in relation to the  creation\n      of micro-business worker cooperatives upon transfer of ownership\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This  bill  would provide assistance in the creation of micro-business\nworker cooperatives upon transfer of ownership of an existing business.\n \n  The State has already  appropriated  funds  for  established  programs\ntailored  to  provide  financial and technical assistance to small busi-\nnesses.  For  example,  the  Entrepreneurial  Assistance  Program  (EAP)\nprovides  instruction,  training  and  technical  assistance  to new and\nmicroenterprises through 24 EAP centers across the state and is  specif-\nically  targeted to provide services in distressed neighborhoods, and to\nmicroenterprises and low-income  individuals;  the  Alternative  Lenders\nProgram  provides support to alternative lenders from community develop-\nment financial institutions, community banks, credit unions and  nonpro-\nfit  organizations to meet the financing needs of micrenterprises; Busi-\nness Mentor NY  helps  microenterprises  grow  through  access  to  free\nbusiness mentors; and finally, the Linked Deposit Program helps existing\nfirms obtain reduced-rate financing so they can undertake investments to\nmake borrowing less expensive.\n \n  Most  recently,  in order to identify additional programs specifically\ntargeted to assist employee-owned enterprises,  the  Advisory  Panel  on\nEmployee-Owned  Enterprises was created by statute just this year (C.30,\nL. 2018). The advisory panel is tasked with  issuing  a  report  on  the\nstate  of  employee  owned  enterprises, which would necessarily include\nmicro-business worker cooperatives, and providing  comprehensive  recom-\nmendations  on  or  before June 13, 2019. It would be premature to enact\nthis legislation prior to being afforded the opportunity to consider the\npanel's work and recommendations.\n \n  I remain fully committed to strategies and programs geared to  support\nsmall  businesses, which are at the heart of building the state's econo-\nmy. However, this bill is flawed in that it would  create  a  new  grant\nprogram  funded  through  the  Economic Development Fund, which is fully\ncommitted, and thus would necessarily  reduce  funding  for  established\nprograms. I have vetoed similar bills in the past which sought to create\ngrant  programs  funded  through  the Economic Development Fund as those\nbills should have been considered in the context of the budget.  So  too\nhere. For the foregoing reasons, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                    (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
    "signedDate": null,
    "signer": "ANDREW M. CUOMO",
    "vetoNumber": 274,
    "vetoType": "STANDARD",
    "year": 2018
